\date{2025-04-01}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Cocone equivalence}
\import{stt-macros}

\p{The map cocone-map is an equivalence #{(A \amalg_C B \to Q) \toeq \rm{Cocone}(S,Q)} }

\proof{

\p{We give a quasiinverse to the cocone-map. For the inverse map we use the [recursor](stt-001E).
To show it is a section, we need for any map #{k \colon A \amalg_C B \to Q} and #{a : A \amalg_C B}, that #{\rm{rec}(\rm{cocone-map}(k))(a) = k(a)}.
We proceed by case analysis.
}

##{
\begin{align*}
   \rm{rec}(k\iota_1,k\iota_2,k\textit{gl})(\iota_1(x)) = k\iota_1(x)    && (\beta_1) \\
   \rm{rec}(k\iota_1,k\iota_2,k\textit{gl})(\iota_2(x)) = k\iota_1(x)    && (\beta_2) \\
   \rm{rec}(k\iota_1,k\iota_2,k\textit{gl})(gl(x)) = k\textit{gl}(x)     && (\beta_{gl})
\end{align*}
}

\p{
, we can verify that #{\rm{rec}(k\iota_1,k\iota_2,kh)(\iota_n(x)) = k\iota_n(x)}, which hold by the
computation rules #{\beta_n}. We also need to show that #{\rm{rec}(-,-,kh)(gl(c)) = k(h(c))}, which is also a trivial application of
the computation law #{\beta_{gl}}.

To show it is a retract, we need to show that for any cocone #{cc = (p,q,h) : \rm{Cocone}(S,Q)},
#{\rm{cocone-map}(\rm{rec}(cc)) = (p,q,h)}. A short calculation verifies that this holds definitionally for the first
and second components, and via the path #{\beta} rule on the third:
}
##{
\begin{align*}
         &\ \rm{cocone-map}(\rm{rec}(cc)) \\
      	\equiv &\ (\rm{rec}(cc)\iota_1, \rm{rec}(cc)\iota_2, \rm{rec}(cc)gl) \\
	= &\ (p,q,h)
\end{align*}
}
}
